// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 1): animals and plants.
ADDART({id:"wombats",t:"Wombats",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐾",w:"Wombat",r:["kangaroo","koalas","australiacountry","tasmania","rodents"],
p:[["Australian diggers","Wombats are sturdy, burrowing marsupials from Australia."],["Cube-shaped droppings","They are the only animals known to produce cube-shaped poo."],["Backward pouch","Their pouch opens toward the back so it does not fill with soil."]],
l:"Wombats are short-legged, muscular marsupials native to Australia. There are three living species: the common wombat of southeastern Australia and Tasmania, the southern hairy-nosed wombat and the critically endangered northern hairy-nosed wombat, which survives in only a few places in Queensland. Wombats are about a meter long and can weigh up to about 35 kilograms. They are expert diggers, using strong claws to build long burrow systems where they shelter from heat and predators. Like koalas, their closest living relatives, they carry their young in a pouch, but a wombat’s pouch faces backward so that soil does not get in while the mother digs. Wombats eat grasses, roots and bark, and have slow metabolisms that help them survive on poor food and little water. Famously, wombats produce cube-shaped droppings, which they stack on rocks and logs to mark their territory.",
s:[["Built for digging","Wombats have broad heads, powerful shoulders and flat claws. A wombat can dig a burrow up to 30 meters long, with several entrances and rooms. Their teeth, like rodents’ teeth, never stop growing, which suits their tough diet of grasses and roots."],["Cube poo","How wombats make cubes puzzled scientists for years. A 2021 study found that the final part of the wombat’s intestine has stiffer and more flexible bands, which shape the droppings into cubes as they dry. The research won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2019, before the full study was published."],["Defense","A wombat’s rear end is covered by a hard plate of cartilage. When a predator such as a dingo or fox follows it into a burrow, the wombat blocks the tunnel with its tough backside. Wombats can also run at up to about 40 kilometers per hour for short distances."],["Conservation","The northern hairy-nosed wombat fell to about 35 animals in the 1980s. Protection of its habitat, predator-proof fences and a second colony started in 2009 have increased the population to more than 300. Common wombats suffer from a skin disease called mange, which wildlife carers treat."]],
k:[["Type of animal","Marsupial"],["Species","Three"],["Native to","Australia"],["Closest relatives","Koalas"],["Famous for","Cube-shaped droppings"]],
d:[["1798","Europeans first record wombats"],["1980s","Northern hairy-nosed wombats drop to about 35"],["2009","Second colony of northern hairy-nosed wombats established"],["2019","Wombat poo research wins an Ig Nobel Prize"],["2021","Study explains how wombats make cubes"]],
s2:[["Burrows as shelters","During the 2019–20 Australian bushfires, stories spread that wombats were herding other animals into their burrows. Scientists said that wombats were not deliberately rescuing other animals, but their deep burrows did shelter some creatures from the flames."],["Ancient giants","Diprotodon, a relative of wombats that lived until about 40,000 years ago, was the largest marsupial ever, as big as a rhinoceros and weighing up to about 2,700 kilograms."]],
sp:["The northern hairy-nosed wombat","One of the world’s rarest mammals, the northern hairy-nosed wombat lives mainly in Epping Forest National Park in Queensland, protected by a fence 20 kilometers long to keep out wild dogs."],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal that raises its young in a pouch"],["Burrow","A tunnel dug as a home"],["Metabolism","The chemical processes that use energy in the body"],["Mange","A skin disease caused by mites"]],
f:["Wombats produce cube-shaped droppings.","A wombat’s pouch opens backward.","Wombats block burrows with their hard rear ends."],
fun:"A wombat can produce about 80 to 100 small cubes of poo in a single night.",
tl:"Ang wombat ay matipunong marsupial mula sa Australia na mahusay humukay ng lungga. Ito lamang ang hayop na kilalang naglalabas ng dumi na hugis-kubo, at paatras ang pagkakabukas ng supot nito."});

ADDART({id:"narwhals",t:"Narwhals",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦄",w:"Narwhal",r:["whale","arctic","climchange","inuit","seals"],
p:[["Unicorns of the sea","Narwhals are Arctic whales; males usually have a long spiral tusk."],["The tusk is a tooth","The tusk is an enormously long tooth with millions of nerve endings."],["Deep divers","Narwhals dive to more than 1,500 meters under Arctic ice."]],
l:"The narwhal is a medium-sized toothed whale that lives year-round in the Arctic waters of Canada, Greenland, Norway and Russia. Narwhals are best known for the long, straight, spiral tusk that grows from the upper jaw of most males and some females. The tusk, which can be up to about 3 meters long, is actually a canine tooth that grows through the upper lip, usually twisting counterclockwise. Scientists think it is used mainly to impress females and compete with other males, and it contains millions of nerve endings that may sense changes in water. Narwhals are about 4 to 5.5 meters long, not counting the tusk, and live in groups called pods. They feed on fish, squid and shrimp, diving to more than 1,500 meters. In medieval Europe, narwhal tusks were sold as ‘unicorn horns’ worth more than gold. Today narwhals are hunted sustainably by Inuit communities and face threats from melting sea ice, shipping noise and changing prey.",
s:[["The tusk","About one in 500 males grows two tusks, and some females grow one. Drone footage filmed in 2017 showed narwhals using their tusks to tap and stun Arctic cod before eating them. The tusk is flexible and can bend about 30 centimeters without breaking."],["Life in the ice","Narwhals spend winters in the dense pack ice of Baffin Bay and Davis Strait, breathing through cracks. In summer they move into ice-free bays and fjords. They are sensitive to changes in ice and rarely leave the Arctic."],["Diving","Narwhals make some of the deepest dives of any marine mammal, staying underwater for up to about 25 minutes. Their muscles are rich in myoglobin, a protein that stores oxygen, and their flexible ribs let their chests compress under pressure."],["Narwhals and people","Inuit have hunted narwhals for thousands of years for food, oil and ivory, and narwhal skin, called maktaaq, is rich in vitamin C. Vikings traded tusks to Europe, where they were thought to cure poisons. The Throne Chair of Denmark, made in the 17th century, is decorated with narwhal tusks."]],
k:[["Type","Toothed whale"],["Habitat","Arctic Ocean"],["Tusk length","Up to about 3 m"],["Tusk is","An overgrown canine tooth"],["Deepest dives","More than 1,500 m"]],
d:[["c. 1000","Vikings trade narwhal tusks to Europe"],["1638","Danish scholar Ole Worm shows ‘unicorn horns’ come from narwhals"],["1662–1671","Danish throne decorated with narwhal tusks built"],["2014","Study finds the tusk is a sensory organ"],["2017","Drones film narwhals stunning fish with tusks"]],
s2:[["Unicorn horns","Medieval Europeans believed powdered ‘unicorn horn’ could detect and cure poison, and kings drank from cups made of narwhal ivory. In 1638 the Danish scholar Ole Worm demonstrated that these horns came from narwhals, though the trade continued."],["Arctic changes","As Arctic sea ice shrinks, killer whales can reach areas where narwhals once found safety, and more ships bring noise that can disturb narwhals’ hearing and feeding. Scientists study how narwhals will cope with these rapid changes."]],
sp:["Narwhal songs","Narwhals communicate with clicks, whistles and knocking sounds and use echolocation to find prey under the ice. Their sounds can travel for kilometers through the water."],
g:[["Tusk","A long, protruding tooth"],["Pod","A group of whales"],["Echolocation","Finding objects by sending out sound and hearing echoes"],["Myoglobin","A protein that stores oxygen in muscles"]],
f:["The narwhal’s tusk is a tooth.","Narwhal tusks were sold as unicorn horns.","Narwhals can dive deeper than 1,500 meters."],
fun:"The name narwhal comes from Old Norse words meaning ‘corpse whale’, possibly because of its mottled gray skin.",
tl:"Ang narwhal ay balyena sa Arctic na kadalasang may mahabang paikot na pangil sa mga lalaki. Ang pangil ay talagang ngipin na may milyon-milyong ugat, at sumisisid ang narwhal nang mahigit 1,500 metro."});

ADDART({id:"lobsters",t:"Lobsters",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦞",w:"Lobster",r:["crustacean","atlantic","fish","animaltesting","oceancurrents"],
p:[["Armored sea animals","Lobsters are large crustaceans with hard shells and ten legs."],["Long-lived","Some lobsters live for more than 50 years."],["From cheap food to luxury","Lobster was once fed to prisoners but is now an expensive delicacy."]],
l:"Lobsters are large marine crustaceans with long bodies, hard outer skeletons and ten legs, related to crabs and shrimp. True clawed lobsters, such as the American and European lobsters, have two large front claws: a heavy crusher claw and a thinner cutter claw. Spiny lobsters, found in warmer seas, have no large claws but long spiny antennae. Lobsters live on rocky, sandy or muddy seafloors, hiding in crevices by day and coming out at night to eat fish, mollusks, worms and sometimes other lobsters. Like all crustaceans, they grow by molting, shedding their old shell and forming a new, larger one. Lobsters can live for decades, and some American lobsters probably exceed 100 years. Their blood is bluish because it uses copper to carry oxygen. Lobster fishing is a major industry in Maine and Atlantic Canada. Scientific reviews have concluded that lobsters can probably feel pain, leading the United Kingdom to recognize them as sentient beings in 2022.",
s:[["Body","A lobster’s body has a head joined to a thorax, covered by a shell called a carapace, and a muscular tail used to shoot backward through the water when escaping. Its eyes are on stalks, and it smells and tastes with hairs on its antennae and legs."],["Molting and growing","A young lobster molts many times a year; adults molt about once a year or less. After molting, the soft lobster hides while its new shell hardens. It can regrow lost claws and legs over several molts."],["Colors","Most living lobsters are greenish-brown, turning red only when cooked because heat releases a red pigment. About one in two million lobsters is bright blue because of a genetic difference, and yellow, orange, split-colored and even white lobsters have been found."],["Lobster as food","In colonial North America, lobsters were so plentiful that they were used as fertilizer and fed to servants and prisoners. Railways and canning in the 19th century spread lobster inland, and it gradually became a luxury food. Many lobster fisheries now limit catches and protect egg-bearing females."]],
k:[["Group","Crustaceans"],["Legs","Ten"],["Blood color","Bluish (copper-based)"],["Lifespan","Can be 50–100 years or more"],["Blue lobsters","About 1 in 2 million"]],
d:[["1600s","Lobsters plentiful along New England coasts"],["1840s","Lobster canning begins in Maine"],["1872","Maine passes early laws to protect lobsters"],["2021","UK review finds evidence that lobsters can feel pain"],["2022","UK law recognizes lobsters as sentient"]],
s2:[["Are lobsters immortal?","A popular internet myth claims lobsters never die of old age. Lobsters do keep growing and produce an enzyme that repairs DNA, but molting takes more and more energy as they grow, and very old lobsters may die during a molt or stop molting and develop shell disease."],["Lobsters and warming seas","Warming waters have shifted American lobster populations north. Southern New England catches collapsed after the 1990s, while catches in Maine and Canada rose, though scientists warn that further warming could reduce them too."]],
sp:["Lobster pots","Lobsters are usually caught in baited traps called pots, lowered to the seafloor on ropes. Escape gaps let small lobsters leave, and in Maine, fishers notch the tails of egg-bearing females, which must then be released if caught again."],
g:[["Crustacean","An animal with a hard shell and jointed legs, like a crab"],["Molting","Shedding an old shell to grow"],["Carapace","The shell covering the head and thorax"],["Sentient","Able to have feelings such as pain"]],
f:["Lobster blood is bluish.","Lobsters turn red when cooked.","About one in two million lobsters is blue."],
fun:"Lobsters taste with their legs and smell with tiny hairs on their antennae.",
tl:"Ang lobster ay malaking crustacean sa dagat na may matigas na balat at sampung paa. Ang ilan ay nabubuhay nang mahigit 50 taon, at dating pagkain ng mga bilanggo bago naging mamahaling pagkain."});

ADDART({id:"reindeer",t:"Reindeer and Caribou",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦌",w:"Reindeer",r:["deer","arctic","tundra","finlandcountry","birdmigr"],
p:[["One species, two names","Reindeer in Europe and Asia and caribou in North America are the same species."],["Both sexes grow antlers","They are the only deer in which females usually grow antlers."],["Herded for thousands of years","Arctic peoples such as the Sámi herd reindeer for food and transport."]],
l:"Reindeer, called caribou in North America, are deer adapted to the cold Arctic and sub-Arctic tundra and forests of Europe, Asia and North America. They belong to a single species, Rangifer tarandus, with several subspecies. Reindeer are the only deer in which both males and females usually grow antlers, which are shed and regrown each year. Thick, hollow-haired coats insulate them from extreme cold, and their broad hooves act like snowshoes and help them dig through snow for food, mainly lichens called reindeer moss. Some caribou herds make the longest land migrations of any mammal, traveling thousands of kilometers each year. In northern Scandinavia, Russia and Mongolia, people including the Sámi, Nenets and Evenki have herded semi-domesticated reindeer for thousands of years for meat, milk, hides and transport. Wild reindeer and caribou populations have declined sharply in many areas because of climate change, habitat loss and disease.",
s:[["Adaptations","Reindeer noses warm cold air before it reaches the lungs. Their eyes change color with the seasons, from gold in summer to blue in winter, which may help them see in the long Arctic night. They can also see ultraviolet light, making lichens and predators stand out against snow."],["Migration","The Porcupine caribou herd of Alaska and Canada travels up to about 2,400 kilometers a year between winter forests and summer calving grounds on the coastal plain. Some herds number hundreds of thousands of animals. Their movements shape the lives of wolves, bears and Indigenous hunters."],["Reindeer herding","Reindeer herding is an important part of the culture of the Sámi people of Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia. Herders follow the animals between seasonal pastures, now often using snowmobiles and GPS. Herding is threatened by mining, wind farms, forestry and warmer winters, when rain freezes over the snow and locks away lichens."],["Declines","Studies have found that many North American caribou herds have declined by more than half since the 1990s or 2000s. Woodland caribou in southern Canada are threatened by logging and roads, which help wolves hunt them."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Rangifer tarandus"],["Antlers","Both males and females"],["Main winter food","Lichens (reindeer moss)"],["Longest migrations","Up to about 2,400 km a year"],["Herded by","Sámi, Nenets, Evenki and others"]],
d:[["c. 3,000 years ago","Reindeer herding develops in northern Eurasia"],["1823","‘A Visit from St. Nicholas’ describes eight flying reindeer"],["1939","Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer created"],["1990s","Many North American caribou herds begin to decline"],["2016","Anthrax outbreak kills thousands of reindeer in Siberia"]],
s2:[["Santa’s reindeer","The poem ‘A Visit from St. Nicholas’ (1823) gave Santa Claus eight flying reindeer. Rudolph, with his glowing red nose, was invented in 1939 for a department store booklet. Male reindeer shed their antlers in early winter, while females keep theirs until spring, so Santa’s antlered reindeer are probably female."],["Clicking knees","As reindeer walk, a tendon slips over a bone in their feet, making a clicking sound. Scientists think it helps herds stay together in snowstorms and darkness."]],
sp:["The Sámi","The Sámi are the Indigenous people of northern Scandinavia and Russia’s Kola Peninsula. In Norway and Sweden, reindeer herding is legally reserved mainly for Sámi people, and herding families have their own earmarks to identify their animals."],
g:[["Tundra","Cold, treeless land in the far north"],["Antler","A bony growth shed and regrown each year"],["Lichen","A partnership of fungus and algae"],["Semi-domesticated","Partly tamed but living mostly free"]],
f:["Reindeer and caribou are the same species.","Female reindeer grow antlers.","Reindeer eyes change color in winter."],
fun:"Reindeer can see ultraviolet light, which makes white wolves and urine trails stand out clearly against the snow.",
tl:"Ang reindeer, na tinatawag na caribou sa Hilagang Amerika, ay usang naangkop sa malamig na Arctic. Ito lamang ang usa kung saan may sungay rin ang babae, at pinapastol ito ng mga Sámi at iba pang taga-Arctic."});

ADDART({id:"llamas",t:"Llamas and Alpacas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦙",w:"Llama",r:["camel","andes","perucountry","inca","domestication"],
p:[["Camels of the Andes","Llamas and alpacas are South American relatives of camels."],["Domesticated long ago","People domesticated them in the Andes thousands of years ago."],["Wool and work","Alpacas are raised for soft fiber; llamas mainly carry loads."]],
l:"Llamas and alpacas are domesticated members of the camel family from the Andes Mountains of South America. Their wild relatives are the guanaco and the vicuña. Llamas are larger, standing about 1.7 to 1.8 meters tall at the head and weighing up to about 200 kilograms, with long banana-shaped ears; they were bred mainly as pack animals. Alpacas are smaller, with short spear-shaped ears, and were bred for their soft, warm fleece. People in the Andes domesticated llamas from guanacos and alpacas from vicuñas about 6,000 to 7,000 years ago. Llamas carried goods across the vast Inca Empire, and both animals provided wool, meat, leather and dung for fuel. Today Peru and Bolivia have most of the world’s alpacas and llamas, and they are also kept on farms and as guard animals around the world. Both animals hum to communicate and can spit when annoyed, usually at each other.",
s:[["Llama or alpaca?","Llamas have long faces, tall curved ears and coarser outer hair. Alpacas have shorter, fluffier faces and dense, fine fleece. There are two breeds of alpaca: the fluffy huacaya and the suri, whose fleece hangs in long silky locks."],["Life at high altitude","Llamas and alpacas live at altitudes of 3,500 to 5,000 meters, where the air is thin and cold. Their blood carries oxygen very efficiently, and padded feet with two toes help them walk on rocky slopes without damaging the ground."],["The Inca and camelids","The Inca kept huge herds of llamas and alpacas, owned largely by the state and religious institutions. Llama caravans carried goods along the Inca road network, and the animals were sacrificed in important ceremonies. Spanish conquest and introduced sheep and diseases caused their numbers to fall sharply."],["Wild relatives","The vicuña, the smallest camelid, has one of the finest natural fibers in the world. Overhunting reduced vicuñas to about 10,000 by the 1960s, but protection and community-managed shearing have brought them back to more than 450,000."]],
k:[["Family","Camelids"],["Native to","Andes of South America"],["Domesticated","About 6,000–7,000 years ago"],["Wild ancestors","Guanaco (llama) and vicuña (alpaca)"],["Main uses","Llamas carry loads; alpacas give fiber"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BC","Llamas and alpacas domesticated in the Andes"],["1400s","Inca Empire relies on llama caravans"],["1532","Spanish conquest begins; camelid numbers fall"],["1960s","Vicuñas reduced to about 10,000"],["1984","Alpacas first exported in large numbers to North America"]],
s2:[["Guard llamas","Farmers in North America, Australia and elsewhere use llamas to guard sheep and goats from foxes, coyotes and dogs. A llama will chase, kick or spit at intruders and often bonds with the flock it protects."],["Llamas and medicine","Llamas and alpacas make unusually small antibodies, called nanobodies. Scientists have studied llama nanobodies as possible treatments for diseases, including COVID-19."]],
sp:["Spitting","Llamas and alpacas spit mainly to settle arguments with each other over food or rank. The ‘spit’ is a smelly mix of saliva and partly digested grass from their stomachs. They rarely spit at people unless mistreated."],
g:[["Camelid","A member of the camel family"],["Fleece","The wool coat of an animal"],["Pack animal","An animal used to carry loads"],["Antibody","A protein that helps fight infection"]],
f:["Llamas and alpacas are relatives of camels.","Alpacas were bred for fiber and llamas for carrying loads.","Llamas are used to guard sheep."],
fun:"Llamas and alpacas share communal dung piles, which makes them easy to house-train.",
tl:"Ang llama at alpaca ay mga kamag-anak ng kamelyo mula sa Andes sa Timog Amerika. Inalagaan ang mga ito libu-libong taon na ang nakalipas; pangunahing tagadala ng karga ang llama, habang pinalalaki ang alpaca para sa malambot na balahibo."});

ADDART({id:"okapis",t:"Okapis",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦓",w:"Okapi",r:["giraffe","drccountry","leopold2","conservation","zebras"],
p:[["The forest giraffe","The okapi looks part zebra, but it is the giraffe’s only living relative."],["Discovered in 1901","Western scientists described it only in 1901."],["Endangered","Okapis live only in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo."]],
l:"The okapi is a shy forest animal of central Africa and the only living relative of the giraffe. It has a dark reddish-brown body, a giraffe-like head, and striking black and white stripes on its legs and rump, which once led people to think it was a kind of zebra. Okapis stand about 1.5 meters tall at the shoulder and weigh up to about 350 kilograms. They live alone in the dense Ituri rainforest and other forests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, feeding on leaves, buds, fruit and fungi, which they strip with a long, blue-black tongue that can reach their own ears. Local people knew the okapi well, but European scientists described it only in 1901. Okapis are endangered: their numbers have declined because of habitat loss, hunting, mining and armed conflict. The Okapi Wildlife Reserve, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, protects part of their range.",
s:[["Stripes and camouflage","The stripes on an okapi’s legs break up its outline in the dappled light of the rainforest, making it hard to see. They may also help a young okapi follow its mother through the forest."],["Giraffe relatives","Like giraffes, okapis have a long prehensile tongue, short skin-covered horns called ossicones on males, and walk by moving both legs on one side at once. Genetic studies show okapis and giraffes split from a common ancestor about 11 million years ago."],["Discovery","In the 1890s, explorer Henry Morton Stanley reported a donkey-like animal in the Congo forest. British official Harry Johnston obtained pieces of striped skin and a skull, and the animal was described as a new species in 1901. It became a symbol of the unknown animals still hidden in the world’s forests."],["Conservation","Okapis were listed as endangered in 2013. The Okapi Wildlife Reserve covers about 14,000 square kilometers, but rangers there face armed groups and illegal gold miners. In 2012, an attack on the reserve’s headquarters killed people and 14 captive okapis."]],
k:[["Closest relative","Giraffe"],["Native to","Democratic Republic of the Congo"],["Habitat","Dense rainforest"],["Described by scientists","1901"],["Status","Endangered"]],
d:[["1890","Stanley reports a mysterious forest animal"],["1901","Okapi described as a new species"],["1992","Okapi Wildlife Reserve established"],["1996","Reserve becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2013","Okapi listed as endangered"]],
s2:[["A national symbol","The okapi is a national symbol of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and appears on its banknotes. World Okapi Day is celebrated on 18 October to raise awareness of the species."],["Hard to study","Because okapis are shy and live in dense forest, scientists rarely see them in the wild. Much of what is known comes from camera traps, droppings and animals in zoos."]],
sp:["A long tongue","An okapi’s tongue is about 30–45 centimeters long. It uses it to strip leaves from branches and to clean its eyes and ears."],
g:[["Ossicone","A skin-covered horn on giraffes and okapis"],["Prehensile","Able to grasp"],["Camera trap","A camera triggered by passing animals"],["Endangered","At high risk of extinction"]],
f:["The okapi is the giraffe’s only living relative.","Okapis were described by scientists only in 1901.","An okapi can lick its own ears."],
fun:"Okapis communicate with infrasound, sounds too low for humans to hear, which helps mothers and calves stay in touch in the forest.",
tl:"Ang okapi ay mukhang may bahagi ng zebra ngunit ito lamang ang buhay na kamag-anak ng giraffe. Inilarawan ito ng mga Kanluraning siyentipiko noong 1901 lamang at nanganganib na ngayon sa mga gubat ng Congo."});

ADDART({id:"pufferfish",t:"Pufferfish",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐡",w:"Tetraodontidae",r:["fishes","coral","poisonousplants","japancountry","seahorse"],
p:[["Balloon fish","Pufferfish inflate themselves with water to scare predators."],["Deadly poison","Many contain tetrodotoxin, one of the strongest natural poisons."],["A risky delicacy","In Japan, specially licensed chefs prepare puffer, called fugu."]],
l:"Pufferfish are a family of about 200 species of fish found mostly in warm seas, with some in fresh and brackish water. When threatened, a pufferfish rapidly gulps water, or air if out of water, into a very stretchy stomach, swelling into a ball several times its normal size that is hard for predators to swallow; many species are also covered in spines. Most pufferfish contain tetrodotoxin, a nerve poison produced by bacteria and concentrated in the liver, ovaries and skin, which can paralyze and kill a person in small amounts and has no antidote. Despite this, puffer meat, called fugu, is a prized delicacy in Japan, where chefs must train for years and pass an exam to prepare it. Pufferfish have strong, beak-like teeth that crack shells of crabs and mollusks, and their teeth keep growing. Male white-spotted pufferfish create elaborate circular sand patterns on the seafloor to attract mates.",
s:[["Inflating","A pufferfish can inflate in a few seconds by pumping water into its stomach, which has folds that unfold like an accordion. Inflating takes energy and is stressful, so they do it only when threatened."],["Tetrodotoxin","Tetrodotoxin blocks signals in nerves, causing numbness, paralysis and breathing failure. It is made by bacteria that pufferfish get from their food; puffers raised on clean food in fish farms are nontoxic. Treatment involves supporting breathing until the poison wears off."],["Fugu","In Japan, fugu is served as thin, translucent slices of raw fish arranged like flower petals, or in hot pots. Licensed chefs remove the poisonous organs carefully. Poisoning cases still happen each year, mostly when people prepare puffers they caught themselves."],["Underwater artists","In 1995, divers in Japan spotted mysterious circular patterns about 2 meters wide on the seafloor. In 2011, scientists discovered that they were made by small male pufferfish, which swim back and forth for days to sculpt ridges in the sand, where females come to lay eggs."]],
k:[["Number of species","About 200"],["Defense","Inflating and spines"],["Poison","Tetrodotoxin"],["Delicacy","Fugu (Japan)"],["Famous behavior","Males build sand circles"]],
d:[["1774","Captain James Cook’s crew poisoned by a puffer"],["1888","Fugu ban lifted in Yamaguchi Prefecture, Japan"],["1909","Tetrodotoxin named by Japanese scientist Yoshizumi Tahara"],["1995","Mysterious sand circles found off Japan"],["2011","Pufferfish identified as the sand-circle builders"]],
s2:[["Cook’s close call","During Captain James Cook’s second voyage in 1774, Cook and two naturalists tasted the liver and roe of a pufferfish caught near New Caledonia. They became numb and weak but survived, while a pig that ate the leftovers died."],["Puffers and porcupinefish","Porcupinefish are close relatives with larger spines that stand up when they inflate. Boxfish and ocean sunfish are also in the same group of fish."]],
sp:["Dolphins and puffers","Dolphins have been filmed gently chewing pufferfish and passing them around, apparently becoming dazed by small amounts of toxin. Scientists debate whether they do it deliberately."],
g:[["Tetrodotoxin","A deadly nerve poison found in pufferfish"],["Brackish","Slightly salty water, like in estuaries"],["Antidote","A medicine that counteracts a poison"],["Fugu","Japanese name for pufferfish as food"]],
f:["Pufferfish inflate with water to scare predators.","Tetrodotoxin has no antidote.","Male pufferfish sculpt circles in the sand."],
fun:"A single pufferfish can contain enough tetrodotoxin to kill about 30 adult people.",
tl:"Ang pufferfish ay lumolobo sa pamamagitan ng paglunok ng tubig upang takutin ang mga kaaway. Marami sa mga ito ang may tetrodotoxin, isa sa pinakamalakas na lason sa kalikasan, ngunit niluluto ito ng mga lisensyadong chef sa Japan bilang fugu."});

ADDART({id:"puffins",t:"Puffins",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐧",w:"Puffin",r:["birds","albatross","iceland","norwaycountry","climchange"],
p:[["Colorful seabirds","Puffins are seabirds with bright, triangular beaks in breeding season."],["Expert divers","They ‘fly’ underwater with their wings to catch small fish."],["Cliff-top burrows","Puffins nest in burrows on islands and sea cliffs."]],
l:"Puffins are small seabirds of the auk family that live in the cold waters of the North Atlantic and North Pacific. There are three species: the Atlantic puffin, the horned puffin and the tufted puffin. Puffins are known for their large, brightly colored beaks, which in the breeding season are striped with orange, yellow and blue-gray; after breeding, the colorful outer plates are shed. Puffins spend most of their lives at sea and come ashore only to breed, gathering in large colonies on islands and cliffs, where pairs dig burrows or use rock crevices. They are strong swimmers, using their wings to ‘fly’ underwater to depths of about 60 meters, and catch small fish such as sand eels, holding several crosswise in their beaks. Pairs often return to the same burrow and partner year after year. The Atlantic puffin is listed as vulnerable because of declining food supplies linked to warming seas and overfishing.",
s:[["Beaks","A puffin’s beak has backward-pointing spines on its tongue and roof, letting it hold a row of fish while catching more. Puffins have been seen carrying more than 60 small fish at once. In 2019, scientists found that puffin beaks glow under ultraviolet light, which other puffins may be able to see."],["Breeding","A puffin pair lays a single egg in a burrow and both parents incubate it for about six weeks. The chick, called a puffling, is fed fish for about six weeks before leaving the burrow at night to fly out to sea alone."],["Colonies","The largest Atlantic puffin colonies are in Iceland, Norway, the Faroe Islands, Newfoundland and Scotland. Iceland hosts about half of the world’s Atlantic puffins. Colonies can number tens of thousands of birds, and many islands are popular with birdwatchers."],["Threats","Warmer seas have changed the numbers and timing of sand eels and other fish, causing breeding failures in some colonies. Rats and other introduced predators raid burrows, and oil spills and storms kill birds at sea. Projects to remove rats from islands have helped puffins return."]],
k:[["Family","Auks"],["Species","Atlantic, horned and tufted puffins"],["Diving depth","Up to about 60 m"],["Chick name","Puffling"],["Atlantic puffin status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["1973","Project Puffin begins restoring puffins to Maine islands"],["1981","Puffins return to breed on Eastern Egg Rock, Maine"],["2015","Atlantic puffin listed as vulnerable"],["2019","Puffin beaks found to glow under ultraviolet light"]],
s2:[["Puffling patrols","In the Westman Islands of Iceland, young pufflings flying out to sea at night are often confused by town lights and land in streets. Each August, local children collect them in boxes and release them by the sea the next morning."],["Project Puffin","Puffins had disappeared from most Maine islands by 1900 because of hunting. From 1973, scientists moved chicks from Newfoundland to Eastern Egg Rock and used decoys and mirrors to attract adults. Puffins began nesting there again in 1981."]],
sp:["Clowns of the sea","Puffins’ colorful beaks, orange feet and waddling walk have earned them nicknames such as ‘clowns of the sea’ and ‘sea parrots’. A group of puffins is sometimes called a circus or an improbability."],
g:[["Auk","A family of diving seabirds"],["Colony","A large group of animals nesting together"],["Puffling","A young puffin"],["Incubate","To keep eggs warm until they hatch"]],
f:["Puffins can carry dozens of fish in their beaks.","Puffin beaks glow under ultraviolet light.","Iceland has about half of all Atlantic puffins."],
fun:"Puffins flap their wings up to 400 times a minute and can fly at about 80 kilometers per hour.",
tl:"Ang puffin ay ibong-dagat na may matingkad at tatsulok na tuka tuwing panahon ng pagpaparami. Lumalangoy ito na parang lumilipad sa ilalim ng tubig upang manghuli ng isda at namumugad sa mga lungga sa isla at bangin."});

ADDART({id:"kiwis",t:"Kiwi Birds",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🥝",w:"Kiwi (bird)",r:["birds","nzcountry","ostrich","invasiveplants","conservation"],
p:[["Flightless birds","Kiwi are small, flightless birds found only in New Zealand."],["Huge eggs","A kiwi egg can be about 20 percent of the mother’s weight."],["National symbol","New Zealanders are nicknamed ‘Kiwis’ after the bird."]],
l:"Kiwi are flightless birds native only to New Zealand. There are five species, about the size of a chicken, with shaggy, hair-like feathers, tiny wings hidden under their plumage and no tail. Unusually for birds, kiwi have nostrils at the tip of their long beaks and find food mainly by smell, probing the ground at night for worms, insects and grubs. Female kiwi lay enormous eggs, up to about 20 percent of their body weight, the largest egg relative to body size of any bird; in most species, the male incubates the egg for around 70 to 90 days. Kiwi evolved in a land without ground mammals, so they have no natural defenses against introduced predators such as stoats, cats, dogs and ferrets, which kill most kiwi chicks. The kiwi is New Zealand’s national symbol, and New Zealanders call themselves ‘Kiwis’. Predator control and protected sanctuaries have helped some kiwi populations grow again.",
s:[["Unusual birds","Kiwi have a low body temperature for birds, about 38°C, heavy bones filled with marrow, and whiskers around their beaks for feeling their way in the dark. Their closest relatives are not the nearby moa but the extinct elephant birds of Madagascar."],["The giant egg","A kiwi egg takes up so much space that the female cannot eat much in the final days before laying. Kiwi chicks hatch fully feathered and live off the yolk for their first week or so before leaving the nest to feed."],["Threats and conservation","Stoats, introduced to New Zealand in the 1880s to control rabbits, are the main killers of kiwi chicks. Operation Nest Egg collects eggs from the wild, hatches them in safety and returns young kiwi once they are big enough to defend themselves. New Zealand aims to remove rats, stoats and possums from the whole country by 2050."],["Kiwi and people","For Māori, the kiwi is a taonga, or treasure, and kiwi feathers were woven into prized cloaks. The bird has appeared on New Zealand coins, stamps and military badges. The fruit called kiwifruit, originally known as Chinese gooseberry, was renamed in the 1950s for export."]],
k:[["Native to","New Zealand only"],["Species","Five"],["Flight","Flightless"],["Egg size","Up to about 20% of the female’s weight"],["Nostrils","At the tip of the beak"]],
d:[["1813","Kiwi first described by European scientists"],["1880s","Stoats introduced to New Zealand"],["1959","Chinese gooseberry renamed ‘kiwifruit’ for export"],["1994","Operation Nest Egg begins"],["2016","Predator Free 2050 goal announced"]],
s2:[["Elephant bird cousins","A 2014 DNA study found that the kiwi’s closest relative was the huge, extinct elephant bird of Madagascar, suggesting that the ancestors of kiwi flew to New Zealand and later lost the ability to fly."],["Kiwi calls","Kiwi are rarely seen but often heard at night. Males make a high-pitched whistle and females a lower, hoarser cry, and pairs call to each other across the forest."]],
sp:["Kiwi sanctuaries","Fenced sanctuaries such as Zealandia in Wellington keep out predators, letting kiwi and other native birds live safely. Since 2022, kiwi have been released into hills around Wellington, where community groups trap predators."],
g:[["Flightless","Unable to fly"],["Incubate","To keep eggs warm until they hatch"],["Stoat","A small predatory mammal related to weasels"],["Taonga","A Māori word for a treasure"]],
f:["Kiwi have nostrils at the tip of their beaks.","Kiwi lay the largest eggs for their size of any bird.","Kiwi are found only in New Zealand."],
fun:"Kiwi are so unusual for birds, with hair-like feathers and whiskers, that they are sometimes called ‘honorary mammals’.",
tl:"Ang kiwi ay maliit na ibong hindi lumilipad na matatagpuan lamang sa New Zealand. Napakalaki ng itlog nito kumpara sa katawan, at tinatawag na ‘Kiwis’ ang mga taga-New Zealand bilang parangal dito."});

ADDART({id:"cicadas",t:"Cicadas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦗",w:"Cicada",r:["insect","metamorph","sound","trees","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Loud insects","Male cicadas make some of the loudest sounds of any insect."],["Underground lives","Young cicadas live underground for years, sucking sap from roots."],["Periodical swarms","Some North American cicadas emerge in vast numbers every 13 or 17 years."]],
l:"Cicadas are insects known for their loud buzzing songs on summer days. There are about 3,000 species worldwide, mostly in warm regions. Male cicadas make sound with drum-like organs called tymbals on their abdomens, which they buckle rapidly in and out; their hollow bodies amplify the clicks, and some species reach about 100 decibels, as loud as a motorcycle. Females are attracted by the songs. After mating, females lay eggs in tree twigs, and the hatched nymphs drop to the ground and burrow underground, where they feed on sap from roots for years. Finally they dig out, climb a tree, shed their skins and emerge as winged adults that live only a few weeks. Most species have life cycles of a few years, but periodical cicadas of eastern North America emerge together every 13 or 17 years in enormous numbers, up to millions per hectare. In 2024, two broods emerged together for the first time since 1803.",
s:[["Making sound","The tymbal is a ribbed membrane that clicks as muscles pull it in and out hundreds of times a second. Each species has its own song. Males often sing together in choruses, and both sexes have ears called tympana to hear them."],["Periodical cicadas","Periodical cicadas spend 13 or 17 years underground and then emerge almost all at once when the soil warms to about 18°C in spring. Scientists think this strategy overwhelms predators: birds and other animals cannot eat them all, so many survive to breed. The prime-number cycles may help them avoid predators and other broods with shorter cycles."],["Broods","Periodical cicada populations are grouped into broods, numbered with Roman numerals, that emerge in different years and regions. Brood X, which emerged in 2021, covered much of the eastern United States. In 2024, Brood XIII (17-year) and Brood XIX (13-year) emerged at the same time in parts of Illinois."],["Cicadas and people","Cicadas do not bite or sting and do little harm to mature trees. They are eaten in parts of Asia, Latin America and by some people in the United States. In ancient China, jade cicadas were placed in the mouths of the dead as symbols of rebirth, and cicadas appear in Greek and Japanese poetry."]],
k:[["Species","About 3,000"],["Sound organ","Tymbal"],["Loudest songs","About 100 decibels"],["Periodical cycles","13 or 17 years"],["Adult lifespan","A few weeks"]],
d:[["1634","European settlers record a periodical cicada emergence"],["1803","Broods XIII and XIX last emerge together before 2024"],["2004","Brood X emerges across the eastern United States"],["2021","Brood X emerges again"],["2024","Two broods emerge together for the first time in 221 years"]],
s2:[["Why prime numbers?","Thirteen and 17 are prime numbers, divisible only by themselves and 1. Some scientists suggest prime-number cycles make it unlikely that cicadas will emerge in the same year as predators with regular population cycles, or that different broods will interbreed often."],["Zombie cicadas","A fungus called Massospora infects some periodical cicadas, replacing parts of their bodies with spores. Infected cicadas keep flying and trying to mate, spreading the fungus to others."]],
sp:["Emergence","On spring nights, cicada nymphs crawl out of holes in the ground, climb tree trunks and split open their skins. The soft, pale adults pump blood into their wings and harden over a few hours, leaving empty shells clinging to bark."],
g:[["Tymbal","A drum-like sound organ in cicadas"],["Nymph","A young insect that looks somewhat like an adult"],["Brood","A group of cicadas that emerge in the same year"],["Prime number","A number divisible only by itself and 1"]],
f:["Cicadas can be as loud as a motorcycle.","Periodical cicadas live underground for 13 or 17 years.","Cicadas neither bite nor sting."],
fun:"In some periodical cicada emergences, there can be more than a million cicadas per acre of land.",
tl:"Ang cicada ay insektong kilala sa malakas na huni tuwing tag-init. Nabubuhay ang mga batang cicada sa ilalim ng lupa nang ilang taon, at may mga uri sa Hilagang Amerika na sabay-sabay lumalabas tuwing 13 o 17 taon."});

ADDART({id:"tomatoes",t:"Tomatoes",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍅",w:"Tomato",r:["fruits","perucountry","mexicocountry","potato","explor"],
p:[["A fruit used as a vegetable","Botanically, tomatoes are berries."],["From the Americas","Tomatoes were domesticated in Mexico from wild plants of the Andes."],["Once feared","Many Europeans once thought tomatoes were poisonous."]],
l:"The tomato is the edible fruit of Solanum lycopersicum, a plant of the nightshade family, which also includes potatoes, eggplants, peppers and tobacco. Wild tomatoes, with tiny fruits, grow in the Andes of western South America; people in Mexico and Central America domesticated larger tomatoes, and the Aztecs called them xitomatl. Spanish explorers brought tomatoes to Europe in the 16th century. At first many Europeans grew them as ornamental plants, partly because their leaves and stems, like those of other nightshades, are toxic, but tomatoes became central to Italian and Spanish cooking by the 18th and 19th centuries. Today tomatoes are among the world’s most important vegetables, with China, India and Turkey the largest producers. Botanically the tomato is a berry, but in 1893 the US Supreme Court ruled that it should be taxed as a vegetable because it is eaten as one. Tomatoes are rich in vitamin C and lycopene, the red pigment.",
s:[["Varieties","There are thousands of tomato varieties, from tiny cherry tomatoes to huge beefsteaks, and in colors including yellow, orange, purple, green and striped. Heirloom varieties are old types passed down through generations, while most supermarket tomatoes are bred to ripen evenly and survive shipping."],["Tomatoes in Europe","The first known European description of the tomato, by Italian botanist Pietro Andrea Mattioli in 1544, called it pomi d’oro, ‘golden apples’. In England and North America, some people avoided tomatoes until the 19th century. Tomato sauce for pasta was first recorded in Italy in the late 17th century."],["Fruit or vegetable?","In the case Nix v. Hedden (1893), the US Supreme Court agreed that tomatoes are botanically fruits but decided that in ordinary language and for import taxes, they count as vegetables, like cucumbers and beans."],["Growing tomatoes","Tomatoes need warmth and sunlight and are grown outdoors and in large greenhouses. The Netherlands produces huge yields in greenhouses using little water. Tomatoes picked green are often ripened with ethylene gas, a natural plant hormone."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Solanum lycopersicum"],["Family","Nightshades"],["Origin","Andes (wild); Mexico (domesticated)"],["Largest producer","China"],["Red pigment","Lycopene"]],
d:[["c. 500 BC","Tomatoes cultivated in Mexico"],["1544","First European description of tomatoes"],["1692","First recorded Italian recipe with tomato sauce"],["1893","US Supreme Court rules tomatoes are vegetables"],["1994","First genetically modified food sold, the Flavr Savr tomato"]],
s2:[["La Tomatina","Every August, the town of Buñol in Spain holds La Tomatina, a festival in which thousands of people throw about 120,000 kilograms of overripe tomatoes at each other in the streets. It began in 1945."],["The poison myth","A popular story claims that wealthy Europeans died from eating tomatoes off pewter plates containing lead. Historians say this is probably a myth, but the tomato’s resemblance to poisonous relatives such as nightshade did make some people suspicious."]],
sp:["The Flavr Savr","In 1994, the Flavr Savr tomato, engineered to soften more slowly, became the first genetically modified whole food sold in stores. It was not a commercial success and was withdrawn after a few years."],
g:[["Nightshade family","A plant family including tomatoes, potatoes and peppers"],["Berry","A fleshy fruit with seeds inside"],["Heirloom","An old variety passed down through generations"],["Lycopene","The red pigment in tomatoes"]],
f:["Tomatoes are botanically berries.","The US Supreme Court ruled tomatoes are vegetables.","Tomato leaves are toxic."],
fun:"The early Italian name for the tomato, pomodoro, means ‘golden apple’, because the first tomatoes in Italy were probably yellow.",
tl:"Ang kamatis ay prutas na ginagamit bilang gulay at nagmula sa Amerika. Inalagaan ito sa Mexico mula sa mga ligaw na halaman ng Andes, at minsang inakala ng mga Europeo na nakalalason ito."});

ADDART({id:"apples",t:"Apples",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍎",w:"Apple",r:["fruits","roses","silk","newton","grapes"],
p:[["A fruit from Central Asia","Apples come from wild forests in Kazakhstan."],["Thousands of varieties","There are more than 7,500 apple varieties."],["Grown by grafting","Apple trees do not grow true from seed, so varieties are copied by grafting."]],
l:"The apple is the fruit of the apple tree, Malus domestica, a member of the rose family and one of the most widely grown fruits in the world. Apples descend mainly from a wild apple, Malus sieversii, that still grows in the mountain forests of Kazakhstan, whose largest city, Almaty, was long thought to mean ‘father of apples’. Traders along the Silk Road carried apples west, where they crossed with European crab apples. Apples have been grown in Europe since ancient Greek and Roman times and were brought to the Americas by European colonists. Because each apple seed produces a tree that differs from its parent, growers keep varieties by grafting cuttings onto rootstocks. There are more than 7,500 known varieties, including Gala, Fuji, Granny Smith and Honeycrisp. China produces about half of the world’s apples. Apples feature in myths and stories from the Garden of Eden to Snow White and Isaac Newton’s famous falling apple.",
s:[["From seed to tree","Apple seeds are genetically mixed, so a tree grown from a Fuji seed will produce different, usually small and sour apples. Nearly all orchard trees are grafted, and the rootstock controls how big the tree grows. Dwarf rootstocks let modern orchards plant thousands of small trees per hectare."],["Johnny Appleseed","John Chapman, known as Johnny Appleseed, planted apple nurseries across the American frontier in the early 1800s. His trees grew from seeds, so most produced sour apples used mainly for cider rather than eating."],["Storing apples","Apples can be stored for months in cold rooms with low oxygen, which slows ripening, so fresh apples are available all year. Cut apples turn brown because enzymes react with oxygen. A genetically engineered non-browning apple was approved in the United States in 2015."],["Apples in stories","In Greek myth, a golden apple started the quarrel that led to the Trojan War. William Tell shot an apple from his son’s head. Isaac Newton said that watching an apple fall helped him think about gravity, and a tree said to be descended from Newton’s still grows at his childhood home."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Malus domestica"],["Family","Rose family"],["Wild ancestor","Malus sieversii (Kazakhstan)"],["Varieties","More than 7,500"],["Largest producer","China"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BC","Apples spread west along trade routes from Central Asia"],["1600s","Colonists bring apple trees to North America"],["1666","Newton watches an apple fall, by his own account"],["1868","Granny Smith apple discovered in Australia"],["1991","Honeycrisp apple released"]],
s2:[["Granny Smith","The Granny Smith apple grew from a chance seedling found by Maria Ann Smith in her orchard near Sydney, Australia, in 1868. Its bright green, tart fruit became famous worldwide."],["An apple a day","The saying ‘An apple a day keeps the doctor away’ comes from a 19th-century Welsh proverb. Apples are a good source of fiber and vitamin C, though a 2015 study found that apple eaters visited doctors about as often as others."]],
sp:["Wild apple forests","The wild apple forests of the Tian Shan mountains in Kazakhstan contain trees with fruit of many sizes, colors and flavors. Scientists study them for genes that could make apples more resistant to disease and climate change."],
g:[["Grafting","Joining a cutting from one plant onto the roots of another"],["Rootstock","The root part of a grafted tree"],["Variety","A distinct type of a plant"],["Orchard","A planting of fruit trees"]],
f:["Apples belong to the rose family.","Apples come from wild forests in Kazakhstan.","Apple trees do not grow true from seed."],
fun:"Apples float in water because about a quarter of their volume is air, which makes the game of bobbing for apples possible.",
tl:"Ang mansanas ay nagmula sa mga ligaw na gubat sa Kazakhstan at may mahigit 7,500 uri. Hindi tumutubo nang pareho sa magulang ang punla nito, kaya kinokopya ang mga uri sa pamamagitan ng grafting."});

ADDART({id:"bananas",t:"Bananas",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍌",w:"Banana",r:["fruits","fungi","trade","cloning","gmos"],
p:[["The world’s favorite fruit","Bananas are among the most eaten fruits on Earth."],["Clones","Nearly all export bananas are genetically identical Cavendish clones."],["A disease threat","A fungus called Panama disease threatens banana farms worldwide."]],
l:"Bananas are long, curved fruits produced by large herbaceous plants of the genus Musa, native to tropical Southeast Asia and New Guinea, where people domesticated them at least 7,000 years ago. Although banana plants look like trees, they have no woody trunk; the ‘stem’ is made of tightly packed leaf bases. Wild bananas are full of hard seeds, but cultivated bananas are seedless and are grown from cuttings, so plants of each variety are genetically identical clones. Bananas include sweet dessert bananas and starchy cooking bananas, often called plantains, which are a staple food for hundreds of millions of people in Africa, Asia and Latin America. India is the largest producer, while Ecuador, the Philippines and Central American countries are major exporters. Almost all exported bananas are of one variety, the Cavendish, which replaced the Gros Michel after a fungus called Panama disease devastated plantations in the mid-20th century. A new strain, Tropical Race 4, now threatens the Cavendish.",
s:[["The banana plant","A banana plant grows from an underground stem called a corm and can reach several meters tall in under a year. It flowers once, producing a bunch of up to 200 bananas in rows called hands, and then dies, replaced by new shoots from the base."],["Gros Michel and Cavendish","Until the 1950s, the main export banana was the Gros Michel, said to be sweeter and creamier. Panama disease, caused by a soil fungus, wiped out plantations in Central America, and exporters switched to the resistant Cavendish."],["Tropical Race 4","A new strain of Panama disease, Tropical Race 4 (TR4), began killing Cavendish bananas in Asia in the 1990s and reached Colombia in 2019. There is no cure, and the fungus survives in soil for decades. Scientists are breeding and genetically engineering resistant bananas, and Australia approved a resistant GM Cavendish in 2024."],["Bananas and history","Large companies such as the United Fruit Company controlled banana growing in Central America in the early 20th century and had great influence over governments, giving rise to the term ‘banana republic’. Workers’ strikes, such as the one that ended in the 1928 Banana Massacre in Colombia, were sometimes violently suppressed."]],
k:[["Genus","Musa"],["Origin","Southeast Asia and New Guinea"],["Main export variety","Cavendish"],["Largest producer","India"],["Main threat","Panama disease (TR4)"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BC","Bananas domesticated in New Guinea"],["1870s","Bananas begin to be shipped to North America"],["1928","Banana Massacre in Colombia"],["1950s","Cavendish replaces the Gros Michel"],["2019","TR4 detected in Colombia"],["2024","Australia approves a TR4-resistant GM banana"]],
s2:[["Radioactive bananas","Bananas contain potassium, a small part of which is naturally radioactive. Scientists sometimes use the ‘banana equivalent dose’ to explain tiny radiation amounts, but eating bananas is perfectly safe because the body keeps potassium levels steady."],["Why bananas curve","Bananas start growing downward but then bend upward toward the light, a response called negative geotropism, which gives them their curved shape."]],
sp:["Plantains","Plantains are starchy bananas eaten cooked: boiled, fried or mashed. In countries such as Uganda, where people eat some of the most bananas in the world, the word for food is closely linked to cooked bananas called matooke."],
g:[["Clone","A genetically identical copy"],["Corm","An underground stem that stores food"],["Plantain","A starchy cooking banana"],["Herbaceous","Having a soft, non-woody stem"]],
f:["Banana plants are not trees.","Most export bananas are Cavendish clones.","Bananas are slightly radioactive."],
fun:"Botanically, bananas are berries, while strawberries are not.",
tl:"Ang saging ay isa sa pinakakinakaing prutas sa mundo at nagmula sa Timog-silangang Asya. Halos lahat ng iniluluwas na saging ay magkakaparehong Cavendish clone, at banta sa mga ito ang fungus na tinatawag na Panama disease."});

ADDART({id:"citrus",t:"Citrus Fruits",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍊",w:"Citrus",r:["fruits","vitamins","sailing","china","indiacountry"],
p:[["Oranges, lemons and limes","Citrus fruits include oranges, lemons, limes, grapefruits and mandarins."],["Ancient hybrids","Most citrus are hybrids of a few wild ancestors from Asia."],["Fighting scurvy","Citrus juice helped sailors prevent scurvy."]],
l:"Citrus is a group of flowering trees and shrubs in the rue family whose fruits include oranges, lemons, limes, grapefruits, mandarins and pomelos. Citrus plants originated in the foothills of the Himalayas in southern China, northeastern India and Myanmar, and in Southeast Asia and Australia. Most familiar citrus fruits are hybrids of a few ancestral species, including the pomelo, the mandarin and the citron; the sweet orange, for example, is a cross between pomelo and mandarin. Citrus fruits have a thick rind with oil glands and juicy segments filled with tiny sacs of juice. They are rich in vitamin C, and in 1747 Scottish naval surgeon James Lind showed in one of the first controlled trials that citrus juice cured scurvy, a disease that killed many sailors. Oranges are the most widely grown citrus, with Brazil, China and India among the largest producers. A bacterial disease called citrus greening has devastated orange groves in Florida.",
s:[["Family tree","Genetic studies published in 2018 traced citrus to the southeastern Himalayas about 8 million years ago. Lemons are a hybrid of citron and sour orange; grapefruit is a cross between pomelo and sweet orange, first found in Barbados in the 18th century."],["Scurvy and the sea","Scurvy, caused by lack of vitamin C, causes bleeding gums, weakness and death. Lind’s trial on HMS Salisbury gave different treatments to sailors with scurvy, and those who ate oranges and lemons recovered fastest. The British navy began issuing lemon juice in 1795, leading to the nickname ‘limeys’ for British sailors."],["Uses","Citrus fruits are eaten fresh and made into juice, marmalade, sweets and drinks. Lemon and lime juice flavor cooking around the world. Oils from the peel are used in perfumes, cleaning products and flavorings, and pectin from the peel thickens jams."],["Citrus greening","Citrus greening, or huanglongbing, is caused by bacteria spread by a tiny insect, the Asian citrus psyllid. Infected trees produce small, bitter, green fruit and eventually die. Since it was found in Florida in 2005, the state’s orange harvest has fallen by more than 90 percent."]],
k:[["Family","Rue family"],["Origin","Southeastern Himalayas and Southeast Asia"],["Ancestors","Pomelo, mandarin, citron and others"],["Rich in","Vitamin C"],["Main disease","Citrus greening"]],
d:[["c. 8 million years ago","Citrus ancestors evolve near the Himalayas"],["c. 300 BC","Citrons reach the Mediterranean"],["1747","James Lind’s scurvy trial"],["1795","British navy issues lemon juice to sailors"],["2005","Citrus greening found in Florida"]],
s2:[["Why oranges are orange","In tropical countries, ripe oranges often stay green because the warm nights do not break down the green chlorophyll in the peel. Oranges turn orange in places with cool nights, and some exporters treat green oranges with ethylene gas to change their color."],["The Valencia and the navel","Navel oranges are seedless and have a second small fruit at the end. All navel oranges descend from a single mutated tree found in Brazil in the 1820s, and cuttings were sent to California in 1873."]],
sp:["Buddha’s hand","Buddha’s hand is a type of citron whose fruit splits into long finger-like segments with almost no pulp. It is valued in China and Japan for its fragrance and is used in temple offerings."],
g:[["Hybrid","The offspring of two different species or varieties"],["Scurvy","A disease caused by lack of vitamin C"],["Rind","The tough outer skin of a fruit"],["Pectin","A substance from fruit used to thicken jams"]],
f:["Sweet oranges are hybrids of pomelo and mandarin.","Citrus juice prevents scurvy.","All navel oranges come from one Brazilian tree."],
fun:"Lemons float in water but limes usually sink, because limes are slightly denser.",
tl:"Kabilang sa citrus ang dalandan, lemon, dayap, suha at mandarin. Karamihan sa mga ito ay hybrid ng ilang ligaw na ninuno mula sa Asya, at nakatulong ang katas ng citrus upang maiwasan ng mga marino ang scurvy."});

ADDART({id:"chilies",t:"Chili Peppers",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🌶️",w:"Chili pepper",r:["spice","mexicocountry","tomatoes","indiacountry","explor"],
p:[["Hot fruits","Chili peppers get their heat from a chemical called capsaicin."],["From the Americas","Chilies were domesticated in Mexico about 6,000 years ago."],["Spread worldwide","After 1492, chilies quickly became central to Asian and African cooking."]],
l:"Chili peppers are the fruits of plants in the genus Capsicum, part of the nightshade family. They were domesticated in Mexico and South America at least 6,000 years ago and were widely used by the Maya and Aztecs. Their heat comes from capsaicin, a chemical that triggers pain receptors in the mouth, causing a burning sensation; birds cannot feel it, so they eat chilies and spread the seeds, while mammals are deterred. After Christopher Columbus brought chilies to Europe, Portuguese and Spanish traders carried them around the world within a century, and they became essential in the cooking of India, Thailand, China, Korea, Hungary and Africa. Chili heat is measured on the Scoville scale, from zero for bell peppers to more than 2 million Scoville heat units for the hottest varieties. Pepper X was named the world’s hottest pepper in 2023. India is the largest producer and consumer of chilies. Capsaicin is also used in pain-relief creams and pepper spray.",
s:[["The Scoville scale","Pharmacist Wilbur Scoville devised his test in 1912, diluting chili extract in sugar water until tasters could no longer detect the heat. Today, heat is measured precisely with laboratory instruments. A jalapeño rates about 2,500–8,000 Scoville heat units, a habanero about 100,000–350,000, and Pepper X about 2.7 million."],["Why chilies burn","Capsaicin binds to a receptor called TRPV1, which normally detects heat, so the brain feels burning without any real temperature change. Scientists David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian shared the 2021 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine partly for discovering how this receptor works. Milk helps cool the burn because casein proteins wash capsaicin away."],["A global spread","Chilies spread from Lisbon and Seville to Africa, India and Southeast Asia in the 16th century. Within decades, Indian cooks had adopted them, and chilies replaced black pepper as the main source of heat in many cuisines. Paprika became the national spice of Hungary."],["Varieties","There are five main domesticated species, including Capsicum annuum, which includes bell peppers, jalapeños and cayenne, and Capsicum chinense, which includes habaneros and many superhot peppers. Despite its name, Capsicum chinense comes from the Americas."]],
k:[["Genus","Capsicum"],["Heat chemical","Capsaicin"],["Domesticated","At least 6,000 years ago"],["Heat scale","Scoville scale (1912)"],["Hottest (2023 record)","Pepper X, about 2.7 million SHU"]],
d:[["c. 4000 BC","Chilies domesticated in Mexico"],["1493","Columbus brings chilies to Spain"],["1500s","Portuguese traders carry chilies to India and Asia"],["1912","Wilbur Scoville devises his heat test"],["2021","Nobel Prize for discovering heat receptors"],["2023","Pepper X named the world’s hottest pepper"]],
s2:[["Why ‘pepper’?","Columbus was searching for black pepper, a valuable spice from India. He called the hot fruits he found in the Caribbean ‘peppers’, and the name stuck, even though chilies are not related to black pepper."],["Birds and chilies","Birds lack the receptor that makes capsaicin burn, so they happily eat hot chilies and spread the seeds in their droppings. Scientists think chilies evolved capsaicin partly to discourage mammals, whose teeth crush seeds."]],
sp:["Bell peppers","Bell peppers, also called capsicums or sweet peppers, belong to the same species as many hot chilies but have a gene that stops them making capsaicin, so they rate zero on the Scoville scale."],
g:[["Capsaicin","The chemical that makes chilies hot"],["Scoville heat unit","A measure of chili heat"],["Receptor","A protein that detects a signal"],["Paprika","A spice made from dried red peppers"]],
f:["Birds cannot taste the heat of chilies.","Milk cools chili burn better than water.","India produces the most chilies."],
fun:"The Bhut Jolokia, or ghost pepper, became so famous for its heat that the Indian army tested it for use in smoke grenades.",
tl:"Ang sili ay nakakakuha ng anghang mula sa kemikal na capsaicin. Inalagaan ito sa Mexico mga 6,000 taon na ang nakalipas, at matapos ang 1492 ay naging mahalaga ito sa lutuing Asyano at Aprikano."});

ADDART({id:"onions",t:"Onions and Garlic",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🧅",w:"Allium",r:["spice","egypt","medicinalplants","plantbased","vitamins"],
p:[["The allium family","Onions, garlic, leeks and chives belong to the genus Allium."],["Ancient foods","Onions and garlic were eaten in ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia."],["Tears and smell","Sulfur chemicals give them their strong flavors and make us cry."]],
l:"Onions, garlic, leeks, shallots and chives are plants of the genus Allium, a group of several hundred species that grow from bulbs and have strong-smelling sulfur compounds. They originated in Central Asia and have been cultivated for at least 5,000 years. Ancient Egyptians fed onions and garlic to pyramid builders and placed them in tombs, including that of Tutankhamun; the Greeks and Romans ate them and believed garlic gave strength. Today onions are among the most widely grown vegetables in the world, used in almost every cuisine, with India and China the largest producers, and China grows about three-quarters of the world’s garlic. When an onion is cut, damaged cells release enzymes that produce a gas called syn-propanethial-S-oxide, which irritates the eyes and causes tears. Garlic’s smell comes from allicin, made when cloves are crushed. Alliums are toxic to dogs and cats.",
s:[["Onions","An onion bulb is made of layers of fleshy leaf bases that store food for the plant. Onions come in yellow, red and white varieties, and sweet onions such as Vidalia have less sulfur. Cooking onions slowly breaks down their sugars, producing sweet, brown caramelized onions."],["Garlic","A garlic bulb, or head, is made of cloves, each of which can grow into a new plant. Garlic is planted from cloves rather than seeds, because most cultivated garlic no longer produces fertile seeds. Black garlic is made by aging garlic at warm temperatures for weeks."],["Why onions make you cry","Onions absorb sulfur from the soil. When cells are broken, an enzyme turns sulfur compounds into an irritating gas that reaches the eyes, where it triggers tears to wash it away. Chilling onions, using a sharp knife or cutting under a fan reduces tears."],["Folklore and medicine","Garlic was used as medicine in many traditions and was believed to ward off evil spirits and vampires in European folklore. Studies suggest garlic may slightly lower blood pressure and cholesterol, though evidence for many claimed benefits is weak."]],
k:[["Genus","Allium"],["Members","Onions, garlic, leeks, shallots, chives"],["Origin","Central Asia"],["Tear gas","Syn-propanethial-S-oxide"],["Largest garlic producer","China"]],
d:[["c. 3500 BC","Onions grown in Egypt and Mesopotamia"],["c. 1323 BC","Garlic placed in Tutankhamun’s tomb"],["1897","Bram Stoker’s Dracula links garlic and vampires"],["2002","Scientists identify the enzyme that makes onions cause tears"],["2018","‘Tearless’ onions go on sale in the United States"]],
s2:[["The tearless onion","In 2002, Japanese scientists identified the enzyme that makes the tear-causing gas. Breeders later developed onion varieties with less of it, and ‘tearless’ onions went on sale in the United Kingdom and United States in the 2010s and 2020s."],["Onions in history","Ancient Egyptians sometimes swore oaths on onions, whose rings symbolized eternity. In the Middle Ages, onions were used to pay rent and given as gifts. American Civil War general Ulysses S. Grant reportedly demanded onions for his troops to prevent illness."]],
sp:["Garlic breath","Garlic breath lasts because sulfur compounds such as allyl methyl sulfide are absorbed into the blood and released through the lungs and skin for hours. Eating raw apples, lettuce or mint can reduce the smell."],
g:[["Allium","The plant group including onions and garlic"],["Bulb","An underground bud with food-storing layers"],["Clove","One segment of a garlic bulb"],["Enzyme","A protein that speeds up chemical reactions"]],
f:["Onions and garlic are toxic to dogs and cats.","China grows about three-quarters of the world’s garlic.","Garlic was placed in Tutankhamun’s tomb."],
fun:"Chives, leeks and onions all belong to the same genus, and their flowers, which form round purple or white balls, are edible.",
tl:"Ang sibuyas, bawang, leeks at chives ay kabilang sa genus na Allium. Kinain na ang mga ito sa sinaunang Egypt, at ang mga kemikal na may sulfur ang nagbibigay ng matapang na lasa at nagpapaluha sa atin."});
